WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:05.451
Did Teri Garr really boldly go off the set to&nbsp;
protest Star Trek's sexist wardrobe choices?

00:00:05.451 --> 00:00:11.280
As groovy as Star Trek: The Original Series&nbsp;
is, the 1960s sci-fi television series hasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.960
always aged well in the years since its original&nbsp;
run. Blatant sexism, both on and off the screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.480
was unfortunately par for the course at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.820
something that one Star Trek alum notably called&nbsp;
out. Appearing in just one episode, actor Teri&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.834
Garr had an experience so uncomfortable&nbsp;
that she may have walked off the set.

00:00:27.834 --> 00:00:32.340
Garr plays Roberta Lincoln in the episode&nbsp;
"Assignment: Earth," secretary to Gary Seven,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:37.620
whose mission is to stop humanity from destroying&nbsp;
itself. The stakes are undoubtedly high, but you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:41.940
wouldn't know it considering the wardrobe note&nbsp;
that showrunner Gene Roddenberry gave Garr. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:46.620
the episode, Garr was already wearing a criminally&nbsp;
short skirt, but Roddenberry insisted that it be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:51.240
shortened by another two inches. According to The&nbsp;
Impossible Has Happened: The Life and Work of Gene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:56.201
Roddenberry, author Lance Parkin recounted that&nbsp;
the actor was so insulted that she walked off set.

00:00:56.201 --> 00:01:00.120
But someone who was there at the time has a&nbsp;
different story. In a conversation captured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:04.920
in Inside Star Trek: The Real Story, assistant&nbsp;
director Robert Justman, recalls the incident.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.400
"Teri understood the machinations&nbsp;
of show business [...] so she didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.360
object when just before her first scene&nbsp;
on stage, Gene went to work on her costume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.137
again. [...] Teri looked embarrassed when&nbsp;
she was called to set for her first shot."

00:01:16.137 --> 00:01:19.980
Whether or not she stormed off set, Garr's&nbsp;
negative experience with Star Trek was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.180
turning point. Although she finished her work on&nbsp;
the episode, she washed her hands of anything to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:28.020
do with the franchise in the future. Even&nbsp;
years later, in an interview with Starlog,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:33.060
Garr slammed the Star Trek universe and its&nbsp;
fans, and even maligned sci-fi as a genre.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:38.361
"We're really something. Star Trek&nbsp;
fans believe that. And so do I."

00:01:38.361 --> 00:01:42.180
Teri Garr's sexist experience at the hands&nbsp;
of Gene Roddenberry speaks to the troubled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:45.960
legacy of the show and its creator.&nbsp;
While elements of the original entry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:49.740
in the Star Trek canon have been praised&nbsp;
for being progressive, the series and its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:54.060
writers have also been criticized for portraying&nbsp;
female characters through a misogynistic lens.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:59.220
There are plenty of examples. In Season 2's "The&nbsp;
Changeling," a robot analyzes the brain of Uhura,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:02.756
only to label her as defective and&nbsp;
nonsensical because she's a woman.

00:02:02.756 --> 00:02:07.440
In the Season 3 episode "Turnabout Intruder,"&nbsp;
Captain Kirk is possessed by his ex-girlfriend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:11.520
only for the team to realize this fact&nbsp;
due to how emotionally volatile he acts.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.540
"You will obey my orders! Or you&nbsp;
will be charged with mutiny!"

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:19.920
Beyond Garr, other people involved with various&nbsp;
chapters of the Star Trek franchise over the years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.900
have commented on its controversial history,&nbsp;
and Roddenberry himself. Actor Kate Mulgrew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.660
who starred as Captain Kathryn&nbsp;
Janeway in Star Trek: Voyager,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:32.760
remarked in a 2019 interview with the Radio Times&nbsp;
that despite Roddenberry's visionary status,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.381
he and his original creation were&nbsp;
still stuck with outdated values.

00:02:36.381 --> 00:02:41.160
"It was extremely misogynist. That's what it&nbsp;
was for years and [Next Generation] followed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:45.540
that to a certain extent. Roddenberry himself was&nbsp;
that way. We are simply going to have to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:50.805
this sensibility, this ideology, and we are, but&nbsp;
it's like moving granite. It takes a long time."

00:02:50.805 --> 00:02:54.405
Mulgrew's Voyager, despite being the&nbsp;
first series led by a female captain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.405 --> 00:02:59.400
also took a few giant steps backwards after the&nbsp;
first few seasons. When Jeri Ryan landed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.200
role as the de-assimilated Borg Seven of Nine, she&nbsp;
had more to handle than just on-set tension with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:08.940
Kate Mulgrew. Her famous corset on the show was a&nbsp;
source of discomfort, especially when she needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:13.668
time to herself. The costume was so elaborate that&nbsp;
she needed help anytime she needed the bathroom.

00:03:13.668 --> 00:03:16.343
Ryan recalled during a 2020 Voyager reunion,

00:03:16.343 --> 00:03:20.700
"If I had to go, it was on the radio,&nbsp;
'Jeri's going. 10-1. That's 20.' Because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:25.320
you know, I had to have somebody dress me and&nbsp;
undress me. [...] It was a 20-minute thing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:29.949
get me undressed, go to the bathroom, get&nbsp;
dressed, go back to set. It was a thing."

00:03:29.949 --> 00:03:33.120
Ryan became so self-conscious that&nbsp;
she even tried limiting food and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:36.720
liquids just so she wouldn't hold up&nbsp;
production. The actor's skin-tight suit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.260
represented an objectification that&nbsp;
Star Trek had been moving away from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:45.240
and was a big reason why Ryan and Mulgrew&nbsp;
notoriously didn't see eye to eye. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:49.560
in her appearance in Star Trek: Picard, Ryan&nbsp;
seemed to have a much more comfortable outfit.

